20.    (Currently Amended) An analyte sensor system comprising:an implantable analyte sensor configured to be inserted into a host and continuously monitor at least one analyte;

a sensor mounting unit, the implantable analyte sensor coupled to the sensor mounting unit;

an adhesive pad at least partially in contact withcoupled to the sensor mounting unit to adhere the sensor mounting unit to a skin surface of the host;

a heating element included in the adhesive pad and positioned to provide heat to the skin surface of the host when the implantable analyte sensor is inserted into the host; and

sensor electronics hardware, wherein the sensor electronics hardware is configured to perform operations comprising:

detecting, by the sensor electronics hardware, that the implantable analyte sensor has been inserted into the host [[by]]based at least onreceiving a signal indicating a change in an electrical property of the implantable analyte sensor; [[and]] responsive to receiving the signal indicating the change in the electrical property of the implantable analyte sensor, triggering, by the sensor electronics hardware, power to be provided to the heating element, thereby providing heat to the skin surface of the host upon detection that the implantable analyte sensor has been inserted into the host, wherein the providing of the power to the heating element occursbeing while the implantable analyte sensor is inserted into the hosti responsive to determining that a relationship between a response signal generated by the implantable analyte sensor and a corresponding concentration of the at least one analyte is non-linear, continuing to provide power to the heating element; and

responsive to determining that the relationship is linear after previously determining that the response signal is non-linear, ceasing to provide power to the heating element.











21.    (Currently Amended) AAn analyte sensor system comprising:a glucosean implantable analyte sensor configured to be at least partially inserted into a host and continuously monitor glucoseat least one analyte;

a sensor mounting unit, the glucoseimplantable analyte sensor coupled to the sensor mounting unit;

an adhesive pad at least partially in contact withcoupled to the sensor mounting unit to adhere the sensor mounting unit to a skin surface of the host;

a heating element included in the adhesive pad and positioned to provide heat to the skin surface of the host when the glucoseimplantable analyte sensor is at least partially inserted into the host, at least a portion of the heating element being positioned outside a perimeter of the sensor mounting unit, and the heating element comprising at least a reactant that reacts in the presence of air to generate heat when the sensor mounting unit is removed from packaging and does not react when the sensor mounting unit is positioned within the packaging, wherein the adhesive pad comprises a material that permits at least the reactant to have continued exposure to air after the adhesive pad adheres the sensor mounting unit to the skin surface of the host, thereby continuing to provide heat to the skin surface of the host after insertion of the glucoseimplantable analyte sensor into the host; and

sensor electronics hardware configured to perform operations comprising:responsive to determining that a relationship between a response signal generated by the glucose sensor and a corresponding concentration of glucose is non-linear, continuing to provide power to the heating element; and

responsive to determining that the relationship is linear after previously determining that the response signal is non-linear, ceasing to provide power to the heating element.



22.    (Previously Presented) The analyte sensor system of  The analyte sensor system of wherein the heating element includes an electrically resistive material.











23.    (Previously Presented) The analyte sensor system of claim 20, wherein the sensor electronics hardware is configured to perform operations comprising:determining whether a break-in period is complete, wherein the break-in period begins immediately following detecting that the implantable analyte sensor has been inserted into the host and in response to receiving the signal indicating the change in the electrical property of the implantable analyte sensor, such that heat is provided to the skin surface of the host upon detecting that the implantable analyte sensor has been inserted into the host and at a beginning of the break-in period;

based at least on determining the break-in period is incomplete, continuing to provide power to the heating element, thereby providing heat to the skin surface of the host;

based at least on determining the break-in period is complete, ceasing to provide power to the heating element.



